92186229253 #edb/ref 1045 "Stability of Numerical Methods" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229253 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186230035 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229253 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049575 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229253 #edb/ref 1050 1500.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229253 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "10505396-7ff3-52ce-80bf-1cab768fe5a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229645 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304246 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229645 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332737" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229645 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229645 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.25$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.5$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=9$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δ)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229645 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ddd8726a-dc80-58fb-bcd2-049555ab4372" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229645 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229645 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.25,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.25)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.25}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.25}| \\\\  & =0.8<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & =0.5<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.5)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.5}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.5}| \\\\  & =0.4<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(9)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+9}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{10}| \\\\  & =0.1<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229652 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186228112 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229652 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229652 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229652 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =4,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}=(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-4Δx|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229652 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6d6b24a2-a25a-5fce-957a-e0f07d99344e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186229652 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-4y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-4{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-4{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-4Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)((1-4Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-4Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-4Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is true.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-4Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-4Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-4Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <4Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.3$ also satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230035 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230036 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230035 #edb/ref 1061 594522 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230035 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c441e4ca-9601-5bd8-98b8-304592a68f49" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230035 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230036 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230037 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230036 #edb/ref 1061 594523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230036 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d9cda7a3-3be2-5ad6-8073-62f51df6f6d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230036 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230037 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230037 #edb/ref 1061 594524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230037 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "34e7c45b-1aa6-5e2a-a0c0-4c70c1a2c90a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230037 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230038 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230039 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230038 #edb/ref 1061 594527 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230038 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b09d6c35-55a3-5805-92dc-cd2b2766a54d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230038 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230039 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230040 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230039 #edb/ref 1061 594531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230039 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e903f273-6080-5032-b3e1-8452a8255818" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230039 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230040 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230041 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230040 #edb/ref 1061 594532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230040 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "64f9d725-bde4-51b6-8bdd-15f6dfc6bbe2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230040 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230185 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230041 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230042 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230041 #edb/ref 1061 594533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230041 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f63a1021-37c7-556c-a328-99fccaab7a5a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230041 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230042 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186230043 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230042 #edb/ref 1061 594537 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230042 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c40d6fad-d59f-58ff-81a3-649a536718a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230042 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186230183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230043 #edb/ref 1061 594538 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230043 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c24c474e-a160-5185-a102-eee0357694fb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230043 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186229161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230152 #edb/ref 1064 "When solving ODEs numerically:\n\n- The solution is advanced in many steps.\n\n- Each step introduces a small error (LTE).\n\n- These errors propagate forward (contributing to GTE).\n\nStability describes how a numerical method responds to these small perturbations.\n\nA method is **stable** if small errors remain controlled as the computation proceeds.\nA method is **unstable** if small errors can grow rapidly, even when the true solution stays well-behaved.\n\nStability determines how local errors turn into global errors: a method may be accurate in a single step, but if it is unstable, the global error can grow without bound.\n\nOne way to study stability is to test a numerical method on the following **test equation**:\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0)=1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0\n$$\n\nThe exact solution of the test equation is $y(x) = e^{-\\lambda x},$ which satisfies $y(x) \\to 0$ as $x \\to \\infty.$\n\nWe say that a numerical method with update formula \n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y\n$$\n\n is **stable** for a given step size $\\Delta x$ if, when applied to the test equation, the numerical solution also satisfies\n\n$$\ny_n \\to 0 \\:\\text{ as }\\: n \\to \\infty.\n$$\n\nTo see how this works in practice, let's analyze the stability of Euler's method." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230152 #edb/ref 1065 14217 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230152 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ac3e5ad1-de8e-5400-81a3-ea12a0eae4b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230152 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230181 #edb/ref 1064 "We will now check the stability of Euler's method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0)=1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that, for an initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_n, y_n) \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nApplying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\lambda {y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\lambda {y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, for stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty.$ Here, since $y_0=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\lambda Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)((1-\\lambda Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(1-\\lambda Δx)^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's define $R(\\lambda\\Delta x) = 1 - \\lambda\\Delta x.$ Then, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1.$ The function $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is called the **stability function** of Euler's method.\n\nExpanding this inequality, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1 \\\\ -1<1-\\lambda Δx & <1 \\\\ -2<-\\lambda Δx & <0 \\\\ 0<\\lambda Δx & <2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.$\n\nThe diagram below shows the exact solution curve with the numerical steps of Euler's method for the cases $\\lambda\\Delta x < 2$ and $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2,$ with $\\lambda > 0$ fixed.\n\n![](images/30/30dc27c192c43aa4fb57ed79f8412b85d106e662ce0416f814621d482d2466ea.png)\n\nNotice that, for $\\lambda\\Delta x < 2,$ the numerical values converge to $0$ (stable), whereas for $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2,$ the values oscillate between $-1$ and $1$ and do not converge to $0$ (unstable).\n\nFor values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 2,$ the numerical solution would grow in magnitude at each step, leading to rapidly diverging values." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230181 #edb/ref 1065 14463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230181 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ee73cf90-ef1e-5e56-b1f5-449c9ca0a73f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230181 #edb/ref 1067 "Stability of Euler's Method" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230182 #edb/ref 1064 "In general, when a numerical method\n$y_{n+1} = y_n + \\Delta y_n$\nis applied to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0,\n$$\n\nwe can write the update in the form\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = R(\\lambda\\Delta x)y_n,\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda\\Delta x)$ is the **stability function** of the method.\n\nFor stability, we require $y_n \\to 0$ as $n \\to \\infty.$ Here, since $y_0=1,$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(R(\\lambda Δx))^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & =(R(\\lambda Δx))^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =(R(\\lambda Δx))^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a geometric sequence, this decays to $0$ as $n\\to\\infty$ if and only if $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1.$\n\nThe set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda\\Delta x)| < 1,$ is called the **stability region** of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda\\Delta x > 0$ is called **absolutely stable**.\n\nWe've seen that Euler's method is *not* absolutely stable, since its stability region is $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.$ But what about the implicit Euler method? Let's find out." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230182 #edb/ref 1065 14464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230182 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b296dcea-4d6f-59ab-8621-78aab4024f7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230182 #edb/ref 1067 "Stability Functions, Stability Regions, and Absolute Stability" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230183 #edb/ref 1064 "A table of some numerical methods, their stability functions, and stability regions is shown below. These results assume the test equation $y' = -\\lambda y$ (where $\\lambda > 0$) with step size $\\Delta x$.\n\n| Numerical Method | Stability Function $R(\\lambda \\Delta x)$ | Stability Region $|R(\\lambda \\Delta x)| < 1$ | Absolutely Stable? |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Euler's | $1 - \\lambda \\Delta x$ | $0 < \\lambda \\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Implicit Euler | $\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Trapezoidal | $\\dfrac{1 - \\lambda \\Delta x/2}{1 + \\lambda \\Delta x/2}$ | All values $\\lambda \\Delta x > 0$ | $\\color{green}\\checkmark$ |\n| Modified Euler | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |\n| Runge-Kutta (RK4) | $1 - \\lambda\\Delta x + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^2}{2} - \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^3}{6} + \\dfrac{(\\lambda\\Delta x)^4}{24}$ | $0 < \\lambda\\Delta x < 2.785\\ldots$ | $\\color{red}\\times$ |" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230183 #edb/ref 1065 14470 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230183 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "58221058-7c32-5e60-97f1-2702853713f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230183 #edb/ref 1067 "Table of Results" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230185 #edb/ref 1064 "Now, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\ny' = -\\lambda y, \\qquad y(0) = 1, \\quad x \\geq 0, \\quad \\lambda > 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that, for an initial value problem $y' = f(x,y), y(x_0) = y_0,$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\ny_{n+1} = y_n + f(x_{n+1},y_{n+1}) \\cdot \\Delta x.\n$$\n\nApplying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\lambda {y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+\\lambda {y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+\\lambda Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(\\lambda\\Delta x) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}.\n$$\n\nThe method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y_n$ converges to $0$ as $n \\to \\infty$ if and only if\n$\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\\right| < 1.$\n\nSince $1 + \\lambda\\Delta x > 1$ for any positive step size $\\Delta x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\left|\\dfrac{1}{1 + \\lambda\\Delta x}\\right| < 1\n$$\n\nfor all $\\Delta x > 0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable!\n\nThe diagram below shows the exact solution curve with the numerical steps of both the explicit and the implicit Euler methods for the value $\\lambda\\Delta x = 2.$ \nWe saw before that the explicit method oscillates between the values $-1$ and $1$ and does not converge to $0.$\n\nConversely, the numerical steps of the implicit Euler method converge to $0$ quite quickly for the same step size!\n\n![](images/08/083b5c0193a2a35648434cae4dcf1820fd47c86202c13a7e7e2e078d45d52bdb.png)\n\nImplicit methods tend to be more numerically stable than explicit methods." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230185 #edb/ref 1065 14484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230185 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1648c2df-353a-5461-abab-2c546f249a47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186230185 #edb/ref 1067 "A Worked Example" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302858 #edb/ref 1080 "I only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302858 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 175921