17] [17592186307682 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307682 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186307167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307682 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "32fa52cb-801f-5620-a1c1-57349a96d831" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307735 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305663 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307735 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306296 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307735 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307735 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306814 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307735 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307735 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307735 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307735 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186307210 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186307735 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "44a67f1b-ab27-5410-8ba8-d0b8071f2993" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308250 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308250 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186305771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308250 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308250 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186306535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308250 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186307051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308250 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308250 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308250 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186306086 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308250 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e69407ce-3a88-501e-b535-736d132d0233" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308441 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186308250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308441 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333340" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308441 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308441 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of Euler's method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),\\,y(0)=3,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda =\\frac{7}{3},$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The update formula can be written as ${y}_{n+1}={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{n}.$\n\nII. The method is stable for $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|>1.$\n\nIII. The method is stable for step size $Δx=0.2.$\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308441 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "86209978-f558-5e50-81ba-e94e00e9e46b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308441 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=3,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of Euler's Method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\frac{7}{3}y,\\,y(0)=3.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ Euler's method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n},{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nNow, let's check our statements:\n\n- Applying Euler's method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-\\frac{7}{3}{y}_{n})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & ={y}_{n}-\\frac{7}{3}{y}_{n}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is false.\n\n- For stability, we require ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty .$ Here, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n} & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n-1} \\\\  & =(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)((1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx){y}_{n-2}) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{2}{y}_{n-2} \\\\  & \\,\\,⋮\\,\\,⋮ \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}{y}_{0} \\\\  & =3{(1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx)}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|<1,$ as stated. Hence, statement II is false.\n\n- Expanding the inequality $|1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx|<1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-1 & <1-\\frac{7}{3}Δx<1 \\\\ -2 & <-\\frac{7}{3}Δx<0 \\\\ 0 & <\\frac{7}{3}Δx<2 \\\\ 0 & <Δx<\\frac{6}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we determine stability for the given step sizes:\n\n- Since $Δx=0.2$ satisfies the above inequality, Euler's method is stable for this value. Hence, statement III is true.\n\n- Since $Δx=0.9$ does not satisfy the above inequality, Euler's method is unstable for this value. Hence, statement IV is true.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"III and IV only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308461 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308461 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333546" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308461 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308461 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =6,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1-6Δx}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.10.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308461 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed443d52-d157-5213-99c2-1ffa8b393121" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308461 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-6y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-6{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+6{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+6Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(6Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}.\n$$\n\nThis shows that the statement I is false.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(6Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+6Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+6Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.10.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334009" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308542 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following step sizes is Euler's Method **stable**, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda >0,$ and step size $Δx?$\n\nI. $Δx=\\frac{1.4}{\\lambda }$\n\nII. $Δx=\\frac{1.25}{\\lambda }$\n\nIII. $Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda }$\n\nIV. $Δx=\\frac{0.75}{\\lambda }$\n\n*Hint: The stability function for Euler's method is given by* $R(\\lambda Δx)=1-\\lambda Δx.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f21f7e32-ddad-5b51-8839-e9c4ba026875" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308542 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$  is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nFor stability, we require that the following condition holds:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(\\lambda Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |1-\\lambda Δx| & <1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this gives\n\n$$\n0<\\lambda Δx<2.\n$$\n\nThis means that Euler's method is stable when applied to the test equation for values in the region $0<\\lambda Δx<2.$\n\nNow, we consider each step size in turn.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.4}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.4.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.4 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{1.25}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=1.25.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<1.25 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{2.5}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=2.5\\ge 2.$ Since this value falls *outside* the given region, Euler's method is *not* stable for this step size.\n\n- For $Δx=\\frac{0.75}{\\lambda },$ we have $\\lambda Δx=0.75.$ Since this value falls *within* the given region,\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0<\\lambda Δx & <2 \\\\ 0<0.75 & <2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEuler's method *must be* stable for this step size.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and IV only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308585 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308585 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308585 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308585 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.95$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=0.4$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=2.3$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308585 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9207ec17-19e6-5c10-b901-32c1b663c7f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308585 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method\n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the Implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.95,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.95)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.95}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.95}| \\\\  & =0.5128…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.4,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.4)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.4}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.7142…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.3,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.3)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+2.3}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{3.3}| \\\\  & =0.3030…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308624 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186307598 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308624 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334466" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308624 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308624 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following values is the trapezoidal method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.15$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=0.8$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=2.9$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=6.2$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the trapezoidal method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308624 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4ca57581-1de5-5ee9-ad10-ddff45b3ad82" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186308624 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method  ${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$ is stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\text{ }\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx)\\,{y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the trapezoidal method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1-\\lambda Δx/2}{1+\\lambda Δx/2}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the trapezoidal method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.15,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.15)| & =|\\frac{1-0.15/2}{1+0.15/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.925}{1.075}| \\\\  & =0.8604…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.8)| & =|\\frac{1-0.8/2}{1+0.8/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{0.6}{1.4}| \\\\  & =0.4285…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=2.9,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(2.9)| & =|\\frac{1-2.9/2}{1+2.9/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-0.45}{2.45}| \\\\  & =0.1836…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=6.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(6.2)| & =|\\frac{1-6.2/2}{1+6.2/2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{-2.1}{4.1}| \\\\  & =0.5121…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls within the stability region, the trapezoidal method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “I, II, III, and IV”.\n\nNote that, since the trapezoidal method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372641 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186372642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372641 #edb/ref 1069 "q-333551" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372641 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372641 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true when testing the stability of the implicit Euler method on the test equation ${y}^{′}=-\\lambda y$ with $y=y(x),y(0)=1,x\\ge 0,\\lambda =1,$ and step size $Δx?$  \n\nI. The stability function is $R(Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+Δx}.$\n\nII. The stability region is $|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1.$\n\nIII. The method is absolutely stable.\n\nIV. The method is **unstable** for step size $Δx=0.50.$\n\n*Hint:* The stability function $R$ is given by ${y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372641 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6ea5569-1b38-5840-af43-7864b3cc3a00" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372641 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nA method that is stable for all possible values $\\lambda Δx>0$ is called absolutely stable.\n\nNow, let's check the stability of the implicit Euler method by applying it to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-y,\\,y(0)=1.\n$$\n\nFirst, for an initial value problem ${y}^{′}=f(x,y),y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},$ the implicit Euler method is given by the update formula\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+f({x}_{n+1},{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx.\n$$\n\nWe now examine our statements:\n\n- Applying the implicit Euler method to the test equation, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{n+1} & ={y}_{n}+(-{y}_{n+1})\\cdot Δx \\\\ {y}_{n+1}+{y}_{n+1}\\cdot Δx & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1}(1+Δx) & ={y}_{n} \\\\ {y}_{n+1} & =\\frac{1}{1+Δx}\\cdot {y}_{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the stability function is \n\n$$\nR(Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+Δx}.\n$$\n\nHence, statement I is true.\n\n- The method is stable when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(Δx)| & <1 \\\\ |\\frac{1}{1+Δx}| & <1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${y}_{n}$ converges to $0$ as $n\\to \\infty $ if and only if\n$|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1.$\nHence, statement II is true.\n\n- Now, since $1+Δx>1$ for any positive step size $Δx,$ we have\n\n$$\n|\\frac{1}{1+Δx}|<1\n$$\n\nfor all $Δx>0.$ So, the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, and statement III is true.\n\n- Finally, because the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for step size $Δx=0.50.$ Hence, statement IV is false.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372642 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372643 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372642 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372644 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372642 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372645 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372642 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372642 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186372647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372642 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372642 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372642 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186372647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372642 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "8a5c4cbe-6fc4-557c-90ae-7db2c270cd63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372643 #edb/ref 1080 "I and IV only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372643 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372643 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a29fb2cb-2dbb-533b-bfbc-35dedefe64c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186372644 #edb/ref 1080 "II and III only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1